│ patch_name               │ aggressive_param_sharing_linear_cnots                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current program has 122 parameters, leading to overfitting and poor accuracy (0.99    │
│                          │ test vs 1.00 in prior programs). Program 2 achieved perfect accuracy with only 32         │
│                          │ parameters by using aggressive parameter sharing and a linear CNOT chain.                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key improvements:                                                                     │
│                          │ 1. **Reduce RX layer**: Use only 2 shared parameters (alternating even/odd qubits)        │
│                          │ instead of 8 independent ones                                                             │
│                          │ 2. **Reduce RY layer**: Use only 2 shared parameters (alternating even/odd qubits)        │
│                          │ instead of 8 independent ones                                                             │
│                          │ 3. **Reduce RZ layer**: Use only 3 shared parameters instead of 4                         │
│                          │ 4. **Simplify entanglement**: Replace the complex two-stage CNOT pattern with a linear    │
│                          │ chain [0-1, 1-2, 2-3, 4-5, 5-6, 6-7, 7-0], which is known to be more efficient            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This reduces parameters from 20 per block → 7 per block, leading to 42 circuit parameters │
│                          │ + 2 readout = 44 total (vs 122 currently). This should:                                   │
│                          │ - Reduce overfitting and improve validation accuracy                                      │
│                          │ - Lower test loss while maintaining or improving test accuracy                            │
│                          │ - Improve parameter efficiency (acc/params)                                               │
│                          │ - Enable faster convergence                                                               │
│                          │ - Better match the successful design of Program 2                                         │
